#
# These rules decide which UNTRACKED paths Git hides. They have no
# effect on a path already in the index, which is why the audit below
# passes --no-index: without it `git check-ignore` consults the index
# and answers "not ignored" for a tracked file a rule really would have
# hidden, turning a genuine defect into a false pass. Sweep with the
# plain form, never with -v, because -v also reports `!` negation
# matches and so prints a line for the correctly visible .env.example.
#
# Git applies the LAST matching pattern, so a `!` negation must follow
# the rule it re-includes, and a directory rule is not interchangeable
# with a wildcard one: `.vscode/` would stop Git descending at all and
# no negation inside could ever fire, whereas `.vscode/*` keeps the
# descent. A leading slash anchors a rule to the repository root, which
# is why /target/, /out/, /logs/ and /blitzy/ carry one and cannot reach
# a same-named directory deeper in the tree. Only the credential rules
# are left unanchored, for the reason given beside them.
#
# No glob here matches a content format this repository tracks: there is
# no *.txt, *.json, *.sql, *.html or *.zip rule, and no bare `build/` or
# `data/` either. Fixed-width parity fixtures are .txt, application
# resources and the Grafana dashboard are .json, the legacy z/OS runtime
# archives under samples/m2 are .zip, and `data` is a real path
# component of app/data - so any of those patterns would silently hide
# shipped content. The extension globs that do appear cover only build
# output, runtime files, private key material and editor state, none of
# which this repository has ever tracked.
#
# Two different tests decide whether a rule belongs here. A rule for
# generated output must name something this project's toolchain
# demonstrably produces, or it is dead. A rule that prevents a secret,
# editor or operating-system file from being committed is preventive, and
# its whole value is firing before the accident, so it is kept even
# though nothing matches it today.
#
# Four categories are deliberately absent. Build reports, because every
# report this build emits already lands under target/ - pom.xml pins the
# JaCoCo and OWASP output to ${project.build.directory} and Surefire and
# Failsafe default there - so a root-level restatement could only drift
# out of step. Container state, because docker-compose.yml declares
# named volumes and mounts tracked files read-only INTO the containers,
# so no compose run writes into the working tree. A wrapper jar, because
# .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties sets distributionType
# only-script and none is ever fetched. Other language stacks, because
# this is a single-module Maven project; personal tooling is
# environment-specific and belongs in `git config core.excludesFile`.
# WHAT MUST NEVER BE IGNORED
#   app/**            the frozen COBOL corpus - 148 tracked files that
#                     serve at once as the parity oracle, the
#                     field-contract source and the traceability anchor
#   samples/**        legacy z/OS build tooling, including two tracked
#                     binary runtime archives under samples/m2
#   diagrams/**       legacy architecture illustrations
#   src/**            Java sources, resources, Flyway migrations, the
#                     validation JSON and the test fixtures and
#                     parity baselines under src/test/resources
#   docs/**, DECISION_LOG.md, TRACEABILITY_MATRIX.md   evidence
#   observability/**, localstack-init/**, Dockerfile,
#   docker-compose.yml, .github/**, .mvn/wrapper/**,
#   .mvn/jvm.config, pom.xml, mvnw,
#   mvnw.cmd, .env.example                             deliverables
#
#   .mvn/jvm.config in particular MUST stay tracked. It is the
#   repository-controlled JVM option that keeps the default Maven
#   invocation warning-free under JDK 25, and both bin/mvn and
#   bin/mvn.cmd read it from the working tree, so hiding it would make
#   the warning-free build a per-developer accident instead of a
#   property of the repository. See the "Repository controlled JVM
#   configuration" note in pom.xml for the full rationale and the
#   condition under which the file stops being needed.
#
#   No glob below matches a content format this repository tracks -
#   there is no *.txt, *.json, *.sql, *.html or *.zip rule - and no
#   bare `build/` or `data/` either. Tracked .txt files are
#   fixed-width parity fixtures and expected-output baselines, tracked
#   .json files are application and observability resources, tracked
#   .zip files are the legacy runtime archives, and `data` is a real
#   path component of app/data,
#   so any of those patterns would silently hide shipped content. The
#   extension globs that do appear cover only build output, runtime
#   files, private key material and editor state, none of which this
#   repository has ever tracked.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED
#   Build reports. Every report this build emits already lands under
#   target/. pom.xml pins the JaCoCo execution data and HTML report and
#   the OWASP report to ${project.build.directory}; Surefire and
#   Failsafe default there; .github/workflows/build.yml uploads exactly
#   target/site/jacoco/**, target/carddemo-*.jar and
#   target/dependency-check/**. Restating those paths at the root would
#   duplicate one rule and could only drift out of step with it.
#
#   Container state. docker-compose.yml declares NAMED volumes -
#   postgres-data, localstack-data, prometheus-data, grafana-data - so
#   Docker holds that data outside the repository, and its only bind
#   mounts are read-only mounts of tracked files INTO the containers.
#   No compose run writes into the working tree.
#
#   A wrapper jar. .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties sets
#   distributionType=only-script, so no maven-wrapper.jar is ever
#   fetched into .mvn/wrapper and no rule is needed to hide one.
#
#   Other language stacks. This is a single-module Maven project with
#   no Gradle, Node, Terraform, Kubernetes or front-end build, and the
#   AWS CLI virtualenv the setup guide provisions lives outside the
#   repository. Personal tooling is environment-specific and belongs in
#   a global excludes file (`git config core.excludesFile`), not here.
#
# HOW TO VERIFY A CHANGE TO THIS FILE
#   git ls-files | git check-ignore --stdin --no-index
#                                        the sweep; MUST print nothing
#   git check-ignore --no-index -v -- PATH   which rule matched a path
#   git check-ignore --no-index -q -- PATH   exit 0 ignored, 1 visible
#   git status --porcelain --ignored=matching   what is hidden right now
#   git diff -- app samples diagrams            MUST stay empty
#
# Never add a glob for a format this repository tracks, and never add a
# bare directory name that can match at arbitrary depth: `data/` would
# hide app/data and `*.zip` the samples runtime archives. Both failures
# are silent - Git reports nothing, the files simply stop reaching a
# fresh clone - and the --no-index sweep is the only check that catches
# them.

# Maven build output. Anchored, because pom.xml declares no <modules>, so
# /target/ is complete and an unanchored rule would also reach
# src/test/resources/target/, which is protected fixture space.
/target/

# ---- MkDocs build output. `mkdocs build` renders the site into this
# ---- directory by default, and mkdocs.yml sits at the repository root,
# ---- so any documentation build lands here unless -d redirects it.
# ---- Anchored for the same reason as /target/ above: the rendered site
# ---- is a root-level artefact, and an unanchored site/ would also match
# ---- any directory of that name nested below. Backstage TechDocs
# ---- renders from the sources named in the mkdocs.yml nav, never from a
# ---- committed copy of this output, so nothing depends on it being
# ---- tracked.
/site/

# ---- A stray class file left beside a source by an ad-hoc `javac`
# ---- invocation, which lands outside target/ and is never wanted.
*.class

# IntelliJ IDEA's own compiler output, written to the project root when
# the IDE builds in place instead of delegating to Maven.
/out/

# JVM fatal-error, replay and heap-dump files. The JVM writes these into
# the working directory, so /target/ does not cover them.
hs_err_pid*
replay_pid*
*.hprof

# Logs written by a local run. Anchored, because a nested logs directory
# would belong to whatever produced it.
/logs/
*.log

# Process-id file written by Spring Boot's ApplicationPidFileWriter.
*.pid

# Local scratch: platform screenshots and recordings, and the ad-hoc
# validation scripts that must never reach a commit.
/blitzy/
blitzy_adhoc_test_*

# Local environment overrides. `.env.*` also matches the template
# .env.example, which must stay tracked, so the negation is required
# rather than defensive. The JWT signing key is environment-indirected
# with no committed default, so real values live only in .env.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Private key material and credential stores. Public certificate formats
# are deliberately absent - *.crt, *.cer, *.pub and *.truststore carry no
# private key, so a public test certificate added later stays visible -
# and id_rsa and id_ed25519 are matched exactly, not as id_rsa*, so the
# .pub half is not hidden too. These are the one place an unanchored rule
# is wanted, because a leaked credential is a hazard at any depth and no
# deliverable carries any of those names.
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_ed25519
.aws/
credentials
secrets/

# IntelliJ IDEA project state, all of it user-local here.
.idea/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws

# VS Code. Only user-local state is hidden: the wildcard form is
# required so Git still descends and the three shareable team files
# survive. settings.json stays ignored as per-developer preference.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/tasks.json

# Eclipse and Spring Tool Suite workspace metadata.
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath
.apt_generated/

# Editor swap and backup files, and the residue `git merge` and
# `git apply` leave behind on a conflict.
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej

# Operating-system metadata. macOS first, then Windows.
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# The frozen legacy corpus is protected by OMISSION, not by negation.
# app/, samples/ and diagrams/ are read-only reference material that
# must survive byte-for-byte and stay visible. Unlike .gitattributes and
# .editorconfig, which close with an explicit override block, this file
# protects those trees by containing no rule that can reach them. A
# `!app/**` line would read as protection while doing nothing at all, in
# just the way that misleads the next reader: a negation only takes
# effect against an earlier pattern that matches, and none above matches
# anything under app/. The guarantee is asserted by the --no-index sweep
# instead, which walks every tracked path and must print nothing.
